  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "No, increasing the number of banks does **not** let one channel's return bandwidth grow without limit.\n\nAccording to the lecture, while banks provide independent storage arrays that allow work in one bank to overlap with preparation in another, the **shared data bus** still limits how much data the channel can return at once [S1, p. 36].\n\nThe maximum (peak) bandwidth of a channel is determined by the transfer rate and the bus width, not by the number of banks [S3, p. 39]. The formula provided is:\n\n**Peak Bandwidth = Transfer Rate × Bus Width / 8** [S3, p. 39]\n\nSince the number of banks is not a variable in this equation, adding more banks improves latency hiding and concurrency but does not increase the peak bandwidth of the single channel.",
   "citations": [],
